// YZC-0119: unquoteString resolved \n \t \r \" \' \\ but not \` or \0, even
// though a bare backtick in a string starts homoiconic interpolation
// (spec 1.10), leaving no way to write a literal backtick without a stray
// backslash surviving into the output. unquoteString also chained separate
// strings.ReplaceAll passes, so an escaped backslash immediately followed by
// a plain letter (e.g. \\n) could be reinterpreted by a later pass as if it
// were that pass's own escape sequence (\\ then n -> \n -> real newline).
// Rewritten as a single left-to-right scan; this covers both.
main: {
    print("back\`tick")
    print("literal backslash-n: \\n")
    print("tab\tend")
    print("quote\" and apos\' and backslash\\")
}
main()
